#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Stand Builder's backend up with one command, by calling the four that do it.

    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two              provision, place, register, supervise
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --activate   ...and start the jobs marked default-on
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --status     what each step reports, changing nothing
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --dry-run    say what would happen, change nothing
    bin/stand-up-backend --shape single|two --check      report only; 1 work left, 2 something refused

## THE OPERATOR NAMES THE SHAPE, AND THIS NEVER FALLS BACK FROM ONE TO THE OTHER

`--shape single` installs Builder as the operator's own account, with no
administrator. `provision-backend` runs with `--no-install`, so a missing
program stops the run with the command that installs it, and nothing reaches
for sudo. On Linux it enables lingering for the operator, so the jobs keep
running after logout; where the machine refuses, it says so and carries on.

`--shape two` installs the two-account shape, which needs the `agents` and
`reviewers` accounts and the privileged half. **It checks for them before any
step runs**, and where one is missing it stops, names what it lacked, and
leaves the machine as it found it. Quietly installing the single-account
pieces instead would hand the operator the shape they did not choose, and the
one that gives up the boundary they chose the other for.

**THIS EXISTS BECAUSE FIVE STEPS IN AN UNENFORCED ORDER IS NOT AN INSTALL.**
Each of the four commands below is complete, refuses rather than half-doing its
job, and names the next one on stdout. That is better than the prose in a shell
comment it replaced, and it is still five things an early adopter on an Azure VM
has to run in the right order, holding two facts nobody wrote down:

- **Provision precedes placement**, because placement builds a venv per deploy
  area and needs a working interpreter and `pip` to do it.
- **Registration and supervision follow placement**, because both point into a
  deploy area. `bin/register-mcp` refuses outright when the area is absent, and
  a job pointed at a missing area undoes the rule that a service serves only from
  its deploy area.

**It calls them; it reimplements nothing.** They already refuse rather than
half-installing, say what they did, and change nothing on a second run. This
inherits those four properties by delegation, and must not become the fifth
place any of them is invented.

## THE INTERPRETER IS THE TRAP THIS CARRIES FOR THE OPERATOR

Job declarations are TOML and `tomllib` arrived in 3.11. Ubuntu 22.04 ships
3.10 as `python3`, and installing 3.11 alongside does **not** change what
`/usr/bin/env python3` resolves to — so `bin/install-jobs` fails under the
interpreter that will run it by default, and `bin/provision-backend`'s closing
text tells the operator to type `python3.11 bin/install-jobs` by hand. That
instruction is correct and it is exactly the kind of thing an installer should
not leave to a person.

**This picks an interpreter and PRINTS the one it picked**, for every step that
does not simply inherit this process. `install-backend` is also given it as
`--python`, because that flag defaults to whatever interpreter happened to
invoke it and a deploy venv built with 22.04's stock 3.10 is a worse starting
point than one built with the 3.11 `provision-backend` just installed.

## EVERY MODE'S EXIT CODE MEANS THE SAME THING

`0` nothing to do or everything done, `1` work is left, `2` something
refused. **That holds for `--check` and for a real run.**

**Under `--dry-run` it cannot hold at all**: no tool reports pending work
through its exit code there, so a `0` says only that nothing crashed.

**Under `--status` it holds for half of them, which is worse than neither.**
`provision-backend` exits `1` for a missing package and `install-backend` for a
deploy area that is missing or unreadable — staleness is a `--check` finding,
not a `--status` one; `register-mcp` and `install-jobs` return `0` whatever
they printed, including over `NOT INSTALLED`. So a `0` from those two
is not a clean bill of health, and this reports what each one SAID rather than
leaning on the code. **A `1` means different things in different modes from the
SAME tool**, which is why `verdict_of` takes the mode and each step carries a
`work_left_modes` tuple read off that tool's own returns.

That divergence is also why the chain stops on **any** non-zero rather than on
`2` alone: a step that dies of an uncaught exception exits `1` and one killed by
a signal is negative, and continuing past either installs onto a machine that
cannot run what comes next.

**A skipped supervision step counts as work left**, because it is. A run that
prints "SUPERVISION IS NOT INSTALLED" and exits `0` is a report contradicting
itself, which is the defect this whole command exists one level up to refuse.

## WHAT A GREEN RUN DOES NOT MEAN

**A green supervise step means the declarations in `jobs/` were rendered and
installed for this machine's supervisor**, and with `--activate` that the
default-on jobs were started and checked once. A fork that ships no
declarations has no `jobs/` directory, or none with a `.toml` in it; when no
`--jobs` is given, this checks the default location first and reports that
the step was skipped, rather than surfacing `no such directory`, which reads
as a misconfiguration the operator caused. **When `--jobs` IS given, the step
runs and `install-jobs` refuses in its own words**, because a path the
operator named and got wrong is exactly the thing they should be told about.

**Installed is not started.** `install-jobs` writes the units; it starts only
the default-on ones, and only with `--activate`, which this passes through.
Without it the jobs are on the machine and not running.

## What it never does

**It writes no credentials of its own.** The Library's job declaration mints
Orient's two and the work-queue tools' one, once each, and no installer
copies or overwrites a credential. It provisions no chat webhooks, Slack's
included, although `agent-platform/install.sh` lists them as a manual step:
an installer that sets up a surface nobody reads is worse than one that omits
it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import pwd
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

PROG = "stand-up-backend"
REPO = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent

# `work_left_modes` NAMES THE MODES IN WHICH THAT TOOL'S `1` MEANS PENDING WORK
# RATHER THAN A CRASH, and it is per-tool because the four disagree. Read off
# each tool's own returns rather than assumed from the flag name, because
# `--status` is where they diverge most:
#
#   provision-backend  `return 1 if (missing and args.check)`      (line 317)
#   install-backend    `--check` 1 when stale (988); `--status`
#                      returns `1 if missing` from status()        (866)
#   register-mcp       `--check` 1 when unready (646); `--status`
#                      returns 0 whatever it printed               (616)
#   install-jobs       `--check` 1 when it would write (main);
#                      `--status` only ever returns 0 (report_status); a REAL
#                      run is `activation(...) or (1 if skipped)`,
#                      so it returns 1 when a live unit differs
#                      from its declaration and was skipped — the
#                      common case — when an activation command
#                      failed, or when a job it activated is still
#                      reported down afterwards                    (activation)
#
# Under `--dry-run` no tool reports pending work through its code at all, so a
# `1` there is an uncaught exception in every case and the mode appears nowhere
# below. `None` means a real run, and only `install-jobs` has a non-crash `1`
# there.
STEPS = (
    {
        "name": "provision",
        "tool": "provision-backend",
        "why": "install what the platform lacks, verified by running each program",
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--check"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "place",
        "tool": "install-backend",
        "why": "put the code where a service can serve it, from a published ref",
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "link",
        "tool": "link-commands",
        "why": "put the core commands where an operator can type their names",
        # **AFTER `place` AND NOT BEFORE IT**, because this links into the area
        # that step creates and refuses rather than writing a link to a file
        # that is not there yet.
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        # Read off this tool's own returns, as the note above requires rather
        # than assumed from the flag names: `--check` returns 1 when a command
        # is unlinked OR when every link exists and the directory is not on
        # PATH (both are work left), and `status()` returns `1 if missing`,
        # where "missing" counts an occupied destination as well as an absent
        # one. Neither returns a non-crash 1 in a real run.
        "work_left_modes": ("check", "status"),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "register",
        "tool": "register-mcp",
        "why": "register the kb and ops MCP servers, so a session has a Library",
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check",),
        "needs_311": False,
    },
    {
        "name": "supervise",
        "tool": "install-jobs",
        "why": "render and install the backend's jobs for this machine's supervisor",
        # `--check` here is REAL, added to install-jobs in this change. Mapped
        # to `--dry-run` it could not answer the question: that mode exits 0
        # whether it would write ten units or none, so `--check` reported `ok`
        # for supervise on a machine where nothing was installed.
        "modes": {"dry-run": "--dry-run", "check": "--check", "status": "--status"},
        "work_left_modes": ("check", None),
        "needs_311": True,
    },
)


class Refused(Exception):
    """Something the operator has to decide about. Never a stack trace."""


SHAPES = {
    "single": "one account, the operator's own, with no administrator",
    "two": "the agents and reviewers accounts and a root-owned privileged half",
}

# What the two-account shape needs before anything is installed for it.
TWO_ACCOUNT_USERS = ("agents", "reviewers")
PRIVILEGED_INSTALLER = Path("bin", "install-privileged")


def _account_exists(name: str) -> bool:
    try:
        pwd.getpwnam(name)
    except KeyError:
        return False
    return True


def two_shape_lacks(repo: Path = REPO, exists=_account_exists) -> list[str]:
    """What the two-account shape needs and this machine lacks, one line each.

    **THE ACCOUNTS ARE ASKED OF THE DIRECTORY, NOT OF `provision-accounts
    --check`**, because that command needs root for every mode and this runs
    as the operator. Whether an account that exists has the right shape is
    `provision-accounts`' question, which the refusal tells the operator to
    ask. The privileged half's installer is looked for in this release, which
    is where a release that cannot install it says so.
    """
    lacks = [
        f"the `{name}` account, which `sudo bin/provision-accounts` creates"
        for name in TWO_ACCOUNT_USERS if not exists(name)
    ]
    if not (repo / PRIVILEGED_INSTALLER).is_file():
        lacks.append(
            f"the privileged half: this release has no `{PRIVILEGED_INSTALLER}` "
            f"to install it with")
    return lacks


def refuse_two_shape(lacks: list[str]) -> Refused:
    listed = "\n".join(f"  - {line}" for line in lacks)
    return Refused(
        f"the two-account shape cannot be installed on this machine. It lacks:\n"
        f"{listed}\n"
        f"Nothing was installed or changed, and the single-account shape was not "
        f"installed in its place. To install as one account instead, run with "
        f"`--shape single`.")


LINGER_REFUSED = (
    "lingering could not be enabled for {user}, so Builder's jobs will stop when "
    "{user} logs out and start again at the next login. Whoever administers this "
    "machine can enable it with `loginctl enable-linger {user}`.")


def lingers_before(step: dict, mode: str | None, shape: str,
                   platform: str = sys.platform) -> bool:
    """Whether to enable lingering before running `step`: just before the jobs
    are installed, in a real single-account run, on Linux. Every reporting mode
    changes nothing, and the two-account shape's jobs run as `agents`."""
    return (step["name"] == "supervise" and mode is None and shape == "single"
            and platform.startswith("linux"))


def enable_linger(user: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """Enable lingering for `user` on Linux, or the sentence saying it was refused.

    **A REFUSAL IS REPORTED, NOT RAISED.** Without lingering the user's jobs
    stop at logout, which is a weaker install rather than a broken one, and a
    machine that refuses it is one the operator may not administer, which is
    the case this shape exists for."""
    try:
        done = run(["loginctl", "enable-linger", user],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return LINGER_REFUSED.format(user=user)
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return LINGER_REFUSED.format(user=user)
    return None


def _has_tomllib(interpreter: str) -> bool:
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            [interpreter, "-c", "import tomllib"],
            capture_output=True, timeout=60, check=False,
        ).returncode == 0
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False


def best_interpreter() -> str | None:
    """An interpreter with `tomllib`, or None — never a refusal from in here.

    Returning None rather than raising is what lets a REPORTING mode say which
    step cannot run and still print the other three. Raising from inside the
    step loop threw the whole report away on the one machine the docstring is
    about: a fresh 22.04 box, first `--dry-run`, before `python3.11` exists.
    """
    if _has_tomllib(sys.executable):
        return sys.executable
    for name in ("python3.11", "python3.12", "python3.13", "python3.14"):
        found = shutil.which(name)
        if found and _has_tomllib(found):
            return found
    return None


def interpreter_for(step: dict, best: str | None) -> str:
    if not step["needs_311"]:
        return sys.executable
    if best is None:
        raise Refused(
            "no interpreter with `tomllib` was found, and job declarations are "
            "TOML. `tomllib` arrived in Python 3.11; Ubuntu 22.04 ships 3.10 as "
            "`python3`, and installing 3.11 alongside does not change what "
            "`/usr/bin/env python3` resolves to."
        )
    return best


def declarations_dir(args) -> Path:
    return args.jobs if args.jobs is not None else REPO / "jobs"


def supervision_skipped(args) -> bool:
    """Whether to skip `install-jobs` rather than let it refuse.

    ONLY FOR THE DEFAULT DIRECTORY. `install-jobs` refuses with `no such
    directory`, which is the wrong thing to show for a fork that ships no
    declarations — that is the fork's choice, not the operator's mistake.
    **But a `--jobs` the operator typed and got wrong IS their mistake**, and
    skipping it would tell them the repository ships no declarations, which is
    false about the path they named. So an explicit `--jobs` always runs the
    step and lets the tool speak for itself.
    """
    if args.jobs is not None:
        return False
    directory = declarations_dir(args)
    return not (directory.is_dir() and any(directory.glob("*.toml")))


def argv_for(step: dict, args, mode: str | None, best: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """The exact command line for one step, from what that tool accepts.

    Each tool takes a different subset, so this is a table rather than a loop:
    passing `--deploy` to `provision-backend`, which has no such flag, would
    make argparse exit 2 and be reported as that step refusing.
    `tools/test_stand_up_backend.py` reads each real tool's own
    `add_argument` calls and asserts every flag named here is one it accepts.
    """
    argv = [interpreter_for(step, best), str(REPO / "bin" / step["tool"])]
    if mode is not None:
        argv.append(step["modes"][mode])
    elif step["name"] == "provision" and args.shape == "single":
        # The single-account shape has no administrator: name what is missing
        # and how to install it, never sudo.
        argv.append("--no-install")
    if step["name"] in ("place", "register") and args.deploy is not None:
        argv += ["--deploy", str(args.deploy)]
    if step["name"] == "place" and best is not None:
        # `--python` defaults to whatever invoked the tool. On 22.04 that is
        # the stock 3.10, and a deploy venv built with it is a worse floor than
        # one built with the interpreter provision just installed.
        argv += ["--python", best]
    if step["name"] == "place" and args.seed_registry:
        argv += ["--seed-registry"]
    if step["name"] == "supervise":
        argv += ["--jobs", str(declarations_dir(args))]
        if args.deploy is not None:
            argv += ["--deploy", str(args.deploy)]
        if args.activate:
            argv += ["--activate"]
    return argv


def run_step(step: dict, args, mode: str | None, best: str | None) -> dict:
    """Run one step, ECHOING its output as it arrives, and keep the exit code.

    **Echoed rather than captured, because the waits are long and silent.**
    `install-backend` prints "building the venv… can take a while — do not
    start a second run" before it does exactly that; captured, that warning
    reaches the operator only after the wait it was meant to cover, while apt
    and pip run silent for minutes.

    The exit code is kept rather than translated: the four tools share one
    convention (0 did it, 1 work left, 2 refused) and a caller here needs to
    tell a refusal from a report.
    """
    try:
        argv = argv_for(step, args, mode, best)
    except Refused as exc:
        # NOT raised out of the loop. A reporting mode must still show the
        # other steps; a real run stops on it like any other refusal.
        return {"step": step, "argv": None, "code": 2, "stdout": "",
                "stderr": str(exc), "unrunnable": True}

    print(f"→ {step['name']}: {' '.join(argv[1:])}")
    lines: list[str] = []
    # STDERR GOES TO A FILE, NOT A SECOND PIPE. Draining stdout to exhaustion
    # and only then reading a stderr PIPE deadlocks: a step that fills the
    # stderr buffer blocks writing, so it never closes stdout, so the loop
    # below never ends and the run hangs for ever. Measured, not reasoned —
    # a step writing 400 KB to stderr hung this command until it was killed.
    # A file has no such buffer, and needs no second thread to drain.
    with tempfile.TemporaryFile("w+", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as errors:
        # `-u` OR THE ECHO IS NOT LIVE. Python block-buffers stdout when it is
        # a pipe, so install-backend's "do not start a second run" warning
        # arrives with everything else once the step ENDS — after the wait it
        # exists to cover. Measured: print, sleep 3, print delivers both lines
        # at 3.0s without it and the first at 0.0s with it.
        child = subprocess.Popen(
            [argv[0], "-u", *argv[1:]], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=errors,
            text=True,
        )
        assert child.stdout is not None
        for line in child.stdout:
            sys.stdout.write(line)
            # FLUSHED, OR THE ECHO IS STILL NOT LIVE. `-u` unbuffers the CHILD;
            # this process block-buffers its own stdout whenever that is a pipe,
            # which is how an operator sees it under `tee`, in CI, or through
            # any wrapper. Measured: without this the early warning arrived at
            # 3.2s of a 3s wait — after the thing it exists to cover.
            sys.stdout.flush()
            lines.append(line)
        child.wait()
        errors.seek(0)
        stderr = errors.read()
    print()
    return {
        "step": step,
        "argv": argv,
        "code": child.returncode,
        "stdout": "".join(lines),
        "stderr": stderr,
        "unrunnable": False,
    }


def verdict_of(result: dict, mode: str | None) -> str:
    """What one step's exit code means, WHICH DEPENDS ON THE MODE IT RAN IN.

    A `1` is an uncaught exception in a real run and a report of pending work
    under `--check`, and the same tool produces both. Labelled by the step's
    name alone, every reporting-mode `1` read as `exit 1` — so on the fresh
    machine this command exists for, `--check` printed `exit 1  provision` and
    `exit 1  place` where the honest word is `work left`, with no stderr to
    explain it, because those tools report missing packages and a stale deploy
    area exactly that way. `STEPS[*]["work_left_modes"]` carries the per-tool
    answer; it stays a crash in a real run.
    """
    code = result["code"]
    if code == 0:
        return "ok"
    if code == 2:
        return "REFUSED"
    if code < 0:
        return f"killed ({-code})"
    if code == 1:
        left = mode in result["step"]["work_left_modes"]
        return "work left" if left else "exit 1"
    return f"exit {code}"


def blocks_from(results: list[dict]) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Each step's own closing words, attributed, VERBATIM and not merged.

    **These are four tools' text, not one list**, and the shape differs: only
    `register-mcp` prints a clean outstanding list after its report.
    `provision-backend` puts a summary line there first, `install-backend
    --check` returns before printing its list at all, and `install-jobs` has no
    such list, so its block is whatever followed its first blank line.

    **They also overlap and can contradict the run.** `provision-backend` says
    supervision is installed separately by `bin/install-jobs`, and
    `install-backend` says the MCP servers are not registered by it — both true
    of those tools alone and both describing steps this command has just run.
    De-duplicating would mean parsing four formats that are free to change, so
    the report says what these blocks are instead of pretending they are one
    curated list.
    """
    out = []
    for result in results:
        text = result["stdout"]
        if "\n\n" not in text:
            continue
        tail = text.split("\n\n", 1)[1].strip()
        if tail:
            out.append((result["step"]["name"], tail))
    return out


def report(results: list[dict], args, stopped: dict | None, best: str | None,
           mode: str | None) -> None:
    print(f"{PROG}: {'macos' if sys.platform == 'darwin' else 'linux'}")
    if best is not None and best != sys.executable:
        did = "would use" if mode is not None else "used"
        print(f"  (supervise {did} {best}, and place was given it as --python)")
    for result in results:
        step = result["step"]
        print(f"  {verdict_of(result, mode):<12} {step['name']:<10} {step['why']}")
    if supervision_skipped(args):
        print(f"  {'not built':<12} {'supervise':<10} "
              f"this repository ships no job declarations — see below")
    print()

    # EVERY refusal's reason, not only the one that stopped the chain. In a
    # reporting mode nothing stops, so `stopped` is None and these would
    # otherwise print `REFUSED place` and never say why.
    for result in results:
        if result["code"] != 0 and (result["stderr"] or "").strip():
            print(f"what `{result['step']['name']}` said:")
            for line in result["stderr"].strip().splitlines():
                print(f"    {line}")
            print()

    if stopped is not None:
        print(f"stopped at the `{stopped['step']['name']}` step "
              f"({verdict_of(stopped, mode)}). Nothing after it ran.")
    elif all(r["code"] == 0 for r in results) and not supervision_skipped(args):
        # ONLY --check EARNS THE WORD SUCCESS, because only there does every
        # step's 0 mean nothing is pending. Under --dry-run no tool reports
        # pending work by its code — register-mcp --dry-run exits 0 while
        # printing that a real run would refuse. Under --status two of the four
        # DO report it (provision and place exit 1), but register-mcp and
        # install-jobs return 0 whatever they printed, install-jobs over NOT
        # INSTALLED, so an all-zero --status still does not mean settled.
        # Saying "reported success" in either mode contradicts the lines above.
        if mode in ("dry-run", "status"):
            print(f"every step ran ({len(results)} of {len(STEPS)}); these modes "
                  f"exit 0 whatever is pending — read the report, not the code")
        else:
            print(f"every step reported success ({len(results)} of {len(STEPS)} ran)")
    else:
        left = [r["step"]["name"] for r in results if r["code"] != 0]
        if supervision_skipped(args):
            left.append("supervise")
        print(f"work left in: {', '.join(left)}")


def outstanding(args, blocks, mode: str | None, supervise_ok: bool) -> list[str]:
    lines: list[str] = []
    if supervision_skipped(args):
        lines += [
            (
                "SUPERVISION IS NOT INSTALLED, AND A BACKEND WITHOUT IT DOES NOT "
                "START. `bin/install-jobs` is complete, and this repository "
                "ships no job declarations, so there is nothing for it to read. "
                "This is not something you have misconfigured:"
            ),
            f"    expected declarations in: {declarations_dir(args)}",
            "",
        ]
    elif not args.activate and mode is None and supervise_ok:
        lines += [
            (
                "The jobs are INSTALLED AND NOT STARTED. `install-jobs` starts the "
                "default-on ones only with `--activate`, which this did not pass:"
            ),
            f"    {PROG} --activate",
            "",
        ]
    if mode == "dry-run":
        lines += [
            (
                "EVERY PREDICTION AFTER THE FIRST STEP IS MADE AGAINST THIS "
                "MACHINE AS IT IS NOW, not against what the earlier steps would "
                "have done. On a fresh machine the `register` step will predict a "
                "refusal, because nothing has been placed for it to point at; a "
                "real run places first and it does not refuse."
            ),
            "",
        ]
    if blocks:
        lines += [
            (
                "Each block below is that tool's OWN closing text, verbatim. They "
                "are not one curated list: they overlap, and one may tell you to "
                "do something this command has already done."
            ),
            "",
        ]
    for name, block in blocks:
        lines.append(f"from the `{name}` step:")
        lines += [f"    {line}" if line.strip() else "" for line in block.splitlines()]
        lines.append("")
    if not lines:
        lines.append("nothing outstanding was reported by any step.")
    return lines


HOST_KEY = Path("/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub")
SSH_ACCESS_GROUP = "com.apple.access_ssh"
# BY ABSOLUTE PATH, NEVER FROM PATH: the fingerprint this prints is what a
# person trusts a host on, and an earlier, writable PATH directory such as
# ~/.local/bin could otherwise supply an `ssh-keygen` that prints an
# attacker's. Both macOS and Debian's openssh-client install it here.
SSH_KEYGEN = "/usr/bin/ssh-keygen"
DSMEMBERUTIL = "/usr/bin/dsmemberutil"


def _run_quiet(argv: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str]:
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return 1, ""
    return done.returncode, done.stdout


def ssh_allows(user: str, run=_run_quiet) -> bool | None:
    """Whether macOS Remote Login lets `user` in: True, False, or None when
    this cannot tell. Remote Login limited to some users keeps them in
    `com.apple.access_ssh`, and an account outside it is refused before any
    password is asked."""
    if sys.platform != "darwin":
        return None
    # `dsmemberutil`, NOT `dscl ... GroupMembership`, because "Only these
    # users: Administrators" is stored as a nested group that GroupMembership
    # never lists: read that way, an administrator who logs in over ssh every
    # day was reported as refused.
    code, out = run([DSMEMBERUTIL, "checkmembership", "-U", user,
                     "-G", SSH_ACCESS_GROUP])
    if code != 0:
        # No such group: Remote Login is open to every account, or is off,
        # which the host key below tells apart.
        return None
    if "is not a member" in out:
        return False
    if "is a member" in out:
        return True
    return None


# FROM PATH, UNLIKE SSH_KEYGEN: the fingerprint is what a person trusts the
# machine on, and the name only says where to look for it, so a false name
# fails at Trust. The Mac app's own command is the fallback, since the app
# puts nothing on PATH until its CLI is installed from its settings.
TAILSCALE_APP_CLI = "/Applications/Tailscale.app/Contents/MacOS/Tailscale"


def tailscale_candidates(which=shutil.which) -> list[str]:
    found = which("tailscale")
    return [p for p in (found, TAILSCALE_APP_CLI) if p]


def tailnet_name(run=_run_quiet, candidates=tailscale_candidates) -> str | None:
    """This machine's MagicDNS name on its Tailscale network, or None
    when Tailscale is absent, stopped, or has MagicDNS off. **A NAME, NEVER A
    100.x ADDRESS:** moving a Mac's Tailscale from the app to the system
    service registers it as a new device with a new address, and a Deck host
    entry holding the old one stops connecting, while the name
    follows the machine."""
    for path in candidates():
        if not os.access(path, os.X_OK):
            continue
        code, out = run([path, "status", "--json"])
        if code != 0:
            continue
        status = json.loads(out)
        if status.get("BackendState") != "Running":
            return None
        # Self.DNSName is the node's name whether or not MagicDNS is on, and
        # with it off the other computer cannot resolve that name.
        if not (status.get("CurrentTailnet") or {}).get("MagicDNSEnabled"):
            return None
        name = ((status.get("Self") or {}).get("DNSName") or "").rstrip(".")
        return name or None
    return None


def pairing_lines(user: str, host_key: Path = HOST_KEY, run=_run_quiet,
                  allows=ssh_allows, name=tailnet_name) -> list[str]:
    """What Deck's Add Host needs from this machine. **THE FINGERPRINT IS THE
    ONE DECK SHOWS AT TRUST**, in the SHA256 form `ssh-keygen -l` prints, so
    the person compares two strings rather than trusting the first answer a
    network gives. Read from the public host key, which every account may
    read, so this needs no administrator."""
    if not host_key.is_file():
        how = ("turn on Remote Login in System Settings › General › Sharing"
               if sys.platform == "darwin" else
               "install and start the ssh server, the openssh-server package")
        return [
            "THIS MACHINE HAS NO SSH HOST KEY, so it accepts no ssh connections and "
            f"Deck on another computer cannot reach it. Ask whoever administers it to {how}.",
        ]
    code, out = run([SSH_KEYGEN, "-lf", str(host_key)])
    fields = out.split()
    fingerprint = fields[1] if code == 0 and len(fields) > 1 else None
    if fingerprint is None:
        return [f"could not read the host key fingerprint from {host_key}."]
    address = name()
    if address:
        lines = [
            "To work from Deck on another computer, add this machine in Deck › Settings › "
            "Hosts by its Tailscale name and this account:",
            f"    address: {address}",
        ]
    else:
        lines = [
            "To work from Deck on another computer, add this machine in Deck › Settings › "
            "Hosts, with the name that computer reaches it by and this account. Use a "
            "name rather than a numeric address, which can change when the machine joins "
            "its network again:",
            "    address: <the name that computer reaches it by>",
        ]
    lines += [
        f"    user: {user}",
        "When Deck asks you to trust the machine, check that it shows exactly:",
        f"    {fingerprint} (ED25519)",
    ]
    if allows(user) is False:
        lines += [
            f"REMOTE LOGIN DOES NOT ALLOW {user} YET, so Deck will be refused. Ask "
            "whoever administers this Mac to add the account under System Settings › "
            "General › Sharing › Remote Login › Allow access for.",
        ]
    return lines


def closing_pairing(euid: int, user: str, shape: str, lines=pairing_lines) -> list[str]:
    """The pairing block, NEVER AS ROOT: the account named is the one Deck
    logs in as, and under sudo it would be root, followed by advice to let
    root in over ssh. Root gets one line saying where pairing comes from."""
    if euid == 0:
        return ["Pairing with Deck is not shown when this runs as root. Run "
                f"`{PROG} --shape {shape} --status` as the account Deck will log in "
                "as, to see the host key fingerprint it should trust."]
    return lines(user)


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description="Stand Builder's backend up by running the four tools in order.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--shape", choices=tuple(SHAPES),
                        help="the install shape, which this never chooses for you: "
                             + "; ".join(f"{k}, {v}" for k, v in SHAPES.items()))
    parser.add_argument("--deploy", type=Path, default=None,
                        help="deploy root, passed to the steps that place and "
                             "read it (default: each tool's own)")
    parser.add_argument("--jobs", type=Path, default=None,
                        help="directory of job declarations (default: <repo>/jobs)")
    parser.add_argument("--seed-registry", action="store_true",
                        help="give the backend the projects registry the published "
                             "release would, not the one a source checkout carries; "
                             "for anyone installing from source but Convivy itself")
    parser.add_argument("--activate", action="store_true",
                        help="also start the jobs marked enabled-by-default")
    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--status", action="store_true",
                      help="what each step reports, changing nothing")
    mode.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                      help="say what would happen, change nothing")
    mode.add_argument("--check", action="store_true",
                      help="report only; 1 if work is left, 2 if a step refused")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    chosen = "status" if args.status else "dry-run" if args.dry_run else \
             "check" if args.check else None

    if args.shape is None:
        shapes = "\n".join(f"  --shape {k:<7} {v}" for k, v in SHAPES.items())
        print(f"{PROG}: name the install shape; this never chooses one for you:\n"
              f"{shapes}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if args.shape == "two":
        # BEFORE ANY STEP, IN EVERY MODE. A reporting mode would otherwise
        # describe installing pieces the two-account shape cannot use.
        lacks = two_shape_lacks()
        if lacks:
            print(f"{PROG}: {refuse_two_shape(lacks)}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    results: list[dict] = []
    stopped = None
    best = None
    linger_note = None
    for step in STEPS:
        # RE-PROBED UNTIL ONE IS FOUND, because `provision` is what installs it.
        # Resolved once before the loop, `best` was None on the very machine
        # this command is written for — a fresh 22.04 box whose `python3` is
        # 3.10 — so `supervise` refused and `place` built its venvs with the
        # stock interpreter, both AFTER provision had just installed 3.11.
        if best is None:
            best = best_interpreter()
        if step["name"] == "supervise" and supervision_skipped(args):
            continue
        if lingers_before(step, chosen, args.shape):
            # The name the process runs as, from its UID. `getpass.getuser()`
            # reads USER and LOGNAME first, which a `su` or `sudo -E` leaves
            # naming someone else.
            linger_note = enable_linger(pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name)
            if linger_note:
                print(f"{PROG}: {linger_note}\n")
        result = run_step(step, args, chosen, best)
        results.append(result)
        # ANY non-zero stops a real run, not only a refusal. A crash exits 1
        # and a signal death is negative, and continuing past either installs
        # onto a machine that cannot run what comes next.
        if result["code"] != 0 and chosen is None:
            stopped = result
            break

    report(results, args, stopped, best, chosen)
    print()
    supervise_ok = any(
        r["step"]["name"] == "supervise" and r["code"] == 0 for r in results
    )
    # Printed again here as well as when it happened, since the steps' output
    # in between scrolls it away and this closing section is what is read.
    if linger_note:
        print(linger_note)
        print()
    for line in outstanding(args, blocks_from(results), chosen, supervise_ok):
        print(line)
    # LAST, since it is the step after this command: pairing Deck with this
    # machine. Read-only, so every mode prints it once the steps have run.
    if stopped is None:
        print()
        for line in closing_pairing(os.geteuid(), pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name, args.shape):
            print(line)

    if stopped is not None:
        print(f"\n{PROG}: the `{stopped['step']['name']}` step said:",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print(stopped["stderr"].strip() or stopped["stdout"].strip(),
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2 if stopped["code"] == 2 else 1
    # ONE MEANING PER CODE, IN EVERY MODE: 2 refused, 1 work left, 0 done.
    # Collapsed to `1 if anything is non-zero`, a refusal and a report of
    # pending work were indistinguishable to a caller.
    if any(r["code"] == 2 for r in results):
        return 2
    if any(r["code"] != 0 for r in results) or supervision_skipped(args):
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
